; The `@name` remedy fixture: every collision message the component face emits names `@name` as the
; fix, and this is the spec that proves the fix works — through the SAME converter and the SAME
; detector the message points at. Both rules here collide WITHOUT their `@name`s
; (`a_rename_remedies_the_collisions_the_detector_messages_name` asserts the un-renamed twins are
; still refused), so the fixture's own generation IS the remedy's proof.

; RESOURCE-level: a record with a field named after its own rule. WIT compares member names against
; the resource's own name, so `a.a` fails component validation; `@name` renames the accessor without
; touching the wire format, and the field is still key `a` on the wire.
a = {
  a: uint, ; @name value
}

; INTERFACE-level: a type choice mints a `<name>-kind` discriminant enum beside its resource, so
; `state` claims BOTH `state` and `state-kind` in the interface's one flat type namespace. A
; group-choice arm wide enough to mint its own struct competes for that namespace, and `@name` is
; what moves it off — here from `state_kind` to `pair`.
state = uint / text

holder = [
  ; @name pair
  tag: 0, x: uint, y: text //
  ; @name single
  tag: 1, z: uint, w: text
]
